From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maicon Alves <alvesmaiconn@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 01:54:12 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 27/27] client: the loading screen can wait for the terrain
 around the player

In singleplayer the loading screen ended once the textures and nodes were ready, and only then did
the server place the player and send the map: the world showed building itself around them, which
looks unfinished. With loading_terrain_radius (nodes, 0 = as before) the screen stays, as "Loading
terrain...", until the server has swept that radius, or the whole view if it is shorter, once (a
full send of its RemoteClient, read through the local server), every block it sent has been
acknowledged, which the client does once it has meshed it, and no mesh is left to make, the
simplified far ones too; or loading_terrain_timeout seconds pass. Blocks the server changes
afterwards and sends again (plants growing, water flowing around the player) don't hold it back.
Until the game starts, the range the client asks for is the radius at most, so the sweep ends there
and nothing farther is meshed meanwhile.

The server sends what is in the player's view cone, so the look has to be the one the game opens
with: TOCLIENT_MOVE_PLAYER now sets the local player's pitch and yaw and its control's (applied each
step, and set only by the game loop), and the game loop starts its camera from them, not from north
for its first frame. Before, a player saved looking any way but north opened onto terrain loaded
behind them. Once the player is placed the camera and the draw list are updated as each frame does,
which is what asks for the far meshes past client_lod_distance.

The steps before it fill the first 60 % of the bar (RenderingEngine::setLoadScreenRange maps a load
screen's 0-100 % into a part of the bar) and the terrain the rest: 90 % of it for how far the sweep
got, then the blocks left to mesh. The sweep starts over when the player moves to another block or
turns, so the bar never goes back. The time taken goes to the log ("Terrain within N nodes loaded
after T s (B blocks, M meshes)"), and at info level, every half second, what it is waiting for.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

